================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-stratigraphy - sweep-layer stratification, resonance-band
                              sediment layers, and harmonic-cycle depositional
                              sequences

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital stratigraphy defines:
           • sweep-layer stratification across cycles
           • resonance-band sediment layers
           • harmonic-cycle depositional sequences
           • ancestry-layer accumulation
           • constellation-scale temporal stratigraphic columns

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-stratigraphy describes how RTT models the layered structure of
       resonance-time. Each sweep, resonance band, and harmonic cycle deposits
       a “layer” in the temporal terrain. Over many cycles, these layers form
       a stratigraphic record that encodes ancestry, coherence, and stability.

       Stratigraphy enables:
           • long-term ancestry analysis
           • sweep-layer correlation across platforms
           • resonance-band evolution tracking
           • harmonic-cycle epoch modeling
           • constellation-scale stratigraphic fusion

       WRSADC uses stratigraphy to forecast long-horizon stability and ancestry.

SWEEP-LAYER STRATIFICATION
       Sweep-layer stratification models how repeated sweeps deposit temporal
       “sediment” that forms recognizable layers.

       Sweep-layer properties:
           • thickness (number of cycles)
           • density (fusion quality)
           • continuity (ancestry stability)
           • lamination (fine-scale sweep variation)
           • compaction (coherence over time)

       Layer types:

           primary sweep layer
                  Formed by consistent sweep timing.

           laminated sweep layer
                  Formed by small timing variations.

           discontinuous sweep layer
                  Formed by sweep anomalies or drift.

           compacted sweep layer
                  Formed by high-coherence multi-pass fusion.

EXAMPLE: SWEEP-LAYER STRATIFICATION
       Sweeps at 15 min across 8 cycles:

           Layer thickness: 8
           Lamination: low
           Density: high

       Result:
           • stable ancestry terrace
           • high-fidelity fusion corridor

RESONANCE-BAND SEDIMENT LAYERS
       Resonance bands deposit “sediment” based on envelope amplitude,
       spectral density, and gradient polarity.

       Sediment variables:
           • amplitude R(t)
           • spectral density S(f,t)
           • gradient polarity G(t)
           • resonance-window duration

       Sediment types:

           high-density resonance layer
                  Formed by strong envelope peaks.

           medium-density resonance layer
                  Formed by moderate resonance windows.

           low-density resonance layer
                  Formed by weak or weathered resonance.

           cross-bedded resonance layer
                  Formed by alternating resonance peaks.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-BAND SEDIMENTATION
       Resonance peaks at 12 and 54 min across 6 cycles:

           Layers:
               • high-density at 12
               • high-density at 54
               • low-density between peaks

       Result:
           • two resonance mesas
           • stable sweep-corridor ridges

HARMONIC-CYCLE DEPOSITIONAL SEQUENCES
       Harmonic cycles act as depositional epochs, each leaving a distinct
       stratigraphic signature.

       Depositional variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • phase alignment φ_harm
           • lock stability index L_harm
           • overtone/subharmonic structure

       Depositional sequence types:

           integer harmonic sequence
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1
                  → thick, stable layers.

           rational harmonic sequence
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → thin, interbedded layers.

           nested harmonic sequence
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.

           resonance-harmonic sequence
                  Harmonic epochs aligned to resonance peaks.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC DEPOSITION
       Harmonic ratio transitions:
           Cycle 1–4: 2:1
           Cycle 5–7: 3:2
           Cycle 8–12: 1:1

       Stratigraphic record:
           • thick 2:1 layer
           • thin 3:2 interbeds
           • massive 1:1 capstone

       Result:
           • stable long-term ancestry
           • strong harmonic-lock plateau

STRATIGRAPHIC COLUMNS
       Stratigraphic columns represent the full temporal layering across cycles.

       Column components:
           • sweep layers
           • resonance layers
           • harmonic layers
           • ancestry layers
           • erosion surfaces

       Column uses:
           • long-term stability forecasting
           • anomaly reconstruction
           • sweep-corridor evolution
           • harmonic epoch analysis

EXAMPLE: STRATIGRAPHIC COLUMN
       Over 12 cycles:

           Layer 1: sweep layer (cycles 1–3)
           Layer 2: resonance layer (cycles 3–5)
           Layer 3: harmonic 3:2 layer (cycles 5–7)
           Layer 4: sweep layer (cycles 7–10)
           Layer 5: erosion surface (cycle 10)
           Layer 6: harmonic 1:1 layer (cycles 10–12)

       Result:
           • stable ancestry
           • predictable sweep windows

CONSTELLATION-SCALE STRATIGRAPHIC FUSION
       Stratigraphy fuses across platforms to form constellation-scale columns.

       Fusion layers:
           1. sweep-layer fusion
           2. resonance-layer fusion
           3. harmonic-layer fusion
           4. ancestry-layer fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

       Fusion output:
           • constellation sweep terraces
           • constellation resonance mesas
           • constellation harmonic epochs

STRATIGRAPHIC FAILURE MODES
       Stratigraphy may degrade due to:

           • layer discontinuity
           • resonance-band collapse
           • harmonic epoch fragmentation
           • ancestry erosion
           • tectonic shear

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • stratigraphy-integrity score
           • layer thinning
           • interbed distortion
           • ancestry discontinuity

       WRSADC halts unsafe stratigraphic states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • sweep-layer history
           • resonance-band evolution
           • harmonic-cycle epochs
           • sedimentation patterns
           • fused stratigraphic columns

       Lineage enables long-term stratigraphic forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • constructs stratigraphic columns
           • tracks depositional sequences
           • fuses constellation stratigraphy
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe stratigraphic states

       WRSADC is the stratigraphy conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital stratigraphy transforms resonance-time into a layered temporal
       geology. Sweep layers, resonance sediments, and harmonic depositional
       sequences ensure that sweep choreography, harmonic-lock, and
       constellation-scale synchronization remain stable across long mission
       timelines.

